# text = The reflections of Sir Mulberry Hawk -- if such a term can be applied to the thoughts of the systematic and calculating man of dissipation , whose joys , regrets , pains , and pleasures , are all of self , and who would seem to retain nothing of the intellectual faculty but the power to debase himself , and to degrade the very nature whose outward semblance he wears -- the reflections of Sir Mulberry Hawk turned upon Kate Nickleby , and were , in brief , that she was undoubtedly handsome ; that her coyness MUST be easily conquerable by a man of his address and experience , and that the pursuit was one which could not fail to redound to his credit , and greatly to enhance his reputation with the world .
